ected by the  record rains  with tens of thousands now living in  emergency shelters  others have been out on the streets  picking through the mud  to see what remains  well correspondent matthias berlinga is  in shangzhou the capital of hanon  province which has been  hit hardest by the flooding mathias  what's the situation there today  so i am now a little bit outside of the  city one of the areas that  was hit first and hardest and i don't  know how well you can see but behind me  this river was overflowing the bridges  that were crossing this river are  destroyed  i am on a road bridge that has survived  that  was more solid and this is the only  place in this whole area where we have  mobile reception  all the other places are still cut off  from the world roads are blocked it's  very hard to access these places  and people are looking at the damage  houses have been damaged people  are telling stories about people about  people that have been  carried away by the floods they still  when they were standing on the street  they still do not have  any information about what happened to  them  it's pretty much a mess here although  the rain has stopped and although as you  can see the water levels  are down now but there is a lot to do  here and there's a lot of things that  will come out after the the there is  more access to the area  now rescue operations are ongoing across  henan province how many people  are in need of help there matthias  it's very hard to say as i told you  there are some places around here that  are not accessible  at all others are accessible but only by  foot because the roads have been  have been swept away so um it's a very  it's it's very hard and  the authorities are not really very open  they're not really disclosing  uh information in a timely manner  um it seems that they are under big  pressure yesterday evening for example  we were in one of the places a tunnel in  in in junzhou city where a lot of people  had  died at that moment or many people were  suspected to die because  many cars were trapped inside the tunnel  um authorities have said two people have  died but  uh this is not very probable that it  will stay at that  death toll the mayor of zhongzhou came  there at midnight  in with the policeman without press and  police were pushing us away she was  having a look at the scene but she did  not  want it to be seen at this place you did  not want to be associated  with what was happening there now i  understand that dams  and rivers are still at risk of  overflowing  their matias are forecasters expecting  more rain  well the situation now i am a little bit  west of jungle and here the rains have  stopped but north of zhengzhou they're  still continuing  and there are still uh uh roads being  flooded  and rivers rising um  we do expect indeed more rain i  am not sure how heavy it will be i think  but it could be quite severe again in  the next two days  this is not over and you can see the  weather it's not bright or anything this  is not gone it's just that the rain  has moved from one place to the other  we're also hearing matthias that the  floods are affecting supply chains  what can you tell us about that  is one of the it is in the central  chinese plane it's a very  important transport hub a lot of lines  run through hernan it is  also one place that the government has  tried to develop as an industrial base  as the coastal regions the traditional  industries  yeah we've got some technical problems  with our line there too china at the  moment that was matthias berlinga  reporting live from zhengzhou in the  center of china's  flood zone
 

